.TH TC 1 "September 2026" "tc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tc \- count tokens (like wc, for tokens)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tc
[\fB\-\-strict\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B tc
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
.br
.B tc
[\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tc
counts tokens in files or standard input using OpenAI's
.B o200k_base
encoding (the encoding used by GPT\-4o and related models).
.PP
With no files,
.B tc
reads standard input and prints the token count.
With one or more files, it prints the count and filename for each;
with more than one file, it also prints a total.
.PP
Behavior mirrors
.BR wc (1):
unreadable files produce an error on stderr, other files continue, and
the exit status is non\-zero if any file failed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version
.RB ( "tc <version>" )
and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat input that is not valid UTF\-8 as an error instead of a warning.
The offending input is not counted and the exit status is non\-zero.
.SH ENCODING OF INPUT
Input is expected to be UTF\-8.
.PP
Input that is not valid UTF\-8 is still counted, using the same substitution
an API client would apply on the way out: each maximal invalid byte sequence
becomes a single U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER. This is the WHATWG rule, and it
matches Python's
.BR "bytes.decode(errors=\(dqreplace\(dq)" ,
so the count agrees with the reference
.B tiktoken
implementation rather than with a naive per\-byte substitution.
.PP
When this happens
.B tc
writes a warning to stderr and the count still goes to stdout, so pipelines are
unaffected. Use
.B \-\-strict
to reject such input instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Count tokens from stdin:
.B echo \-n "hello" | tc
.TP
Count tokens in one file:
.B tc README.md
.TP
Count tokens in multiple files (with total):
.B tc a.txt b.txt
.TP
Pipe a file:
.B cat notes.txt | tc
.TP
Fail rather than guess at non\-UTF\-8 input:
.B tc \-\-strict *.md
.SH ENCODING
v1 always uses
.BR o200k_base .
A future
.B \-e
/
.B \-\-encoding
flag may select other encodings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Omar
